OnScreen: dovi_rpu bsf - convert_p81, Dolby Vision profile 7 -> 8.1

Adds `-bsf:v dovi_rpu=convert_p81=1`. On a profile 7 HEVC stream (UHD
Blu-ray dual layer, FEL or MEL) it drops every enhancement layer NAL
(type 63) and rewrites each RPU the way `dovi_tool -m 2 convert --discard`
does (residual off, NLQ removed, identity BL->VDR mapping for a FEL, the
BT.2020 profile 8.1 DM matrices), and sets the configuration record to
profile 8, compatibility id 1, no EL, uncompressed. One pass, so packet
timestamps (B-frame reordering) stay intact, which a dovi_tool pipe loses.

The conversion walks the packet's NAL units in their own format (hvcC
length-prefixed or Annex B) and copies everything but the EL and RPU byte
for byte, extradata untouched. It deliberately avoids the CBS round trip the
filter's other modes use: that writes Annex B, and an MP4 muxer with an hvc1
tag then moves every in-band parameter set into the sample entry, which
corrupts a base layer whose in-band sets differ from its hvcC ones.

On anything else (profile 8 or 5, no configuration record, AV1) the option
makes the filter a no-op: packets pass through unchanged.

Detect it with: ffmpeg -h bsf=dovi_rpu   (lists "convert_p81")

--- FFmpeg.orig/libavcodec/bsf/dovi_rpu.c
+++ FFmpeg/libavcodec/bsf/dovi_rpu.c
